Experiment 4:  Develop a program to control a DC motor with Arduino board

Algorithm
1.	Start. 
2.	Initialize motor control pins (IN1 and IN2). 
3.	Configure the pins as OUTPUT. 
4.	Rotate the motor clockwise. 
5.	Wait for 3 seconds. 
6.	Stop the motor. 
7.	Wait for 1 second. 
8.	Rotate the motor anticlockwise. 
9.	Wait for 3 seconds. 
10.	Stop the motor. 
11.	Repeat the process continuously.

Line-by-Line Program Explanation
const int IN1 = 8;          // Connect motor driver IN1 to Arduino Pin 8.

const int IN2 = 9;          // Connect motor driver IN2 to Arduino Pin 9.

pinMode(IN1, OUTPUT);       // Configure IN1 as output.

pinMode(IN2, OUTPUT);       // Configure IN2 as output.

digitalWrite(IN1, HIGH);    // IN1 HIGH

digitalWrite(IN2, LOW);     // IN2 LOW
                             // Motor rotates clockwise.

delay(3000);                // Run motor for 3 seconds.

digitalWrite(IN1, LOW);     // Stop motor.

digitalWrite(IN2, LOW);

delay(1000);                // Wait for 1 second.

digitalWrite(IN1, LOW);     // IN1 LOW

digitalWrite(IN2, HIGH);    // IN2 HIGH
                             // Motor rotates anticlockwise.

delay(3000);                // Run motor for 3 seconds.

digitalWrite(IN1, LOW);     // Stop motor.

digitalWrite(IN2, LOW);

delay(1000);                // Wait for 1 second.

loop();                     // The sequence repeats automatically.
Working
•	The Arduino sends control signals to the L293D motor driver. 
•	When IN1 = HIGH and IN2 = LOW, the motor rotates clockwise. 
•	When both inputs are LOW, the motor stops. 
•	When IN1 = LOW and IN2 = HIGH, the motor rotates anticlockwise. 
•	The sequence repeats continuously. 
